---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: eprfs-core-tree-boundary
purpose: >
  Artifact-neutral addressing and filesystem tree contracts. The shared tree is an
  immutable, versioned content-addressed value; local projection state remains rebuildable.
  The view-scoped walk and the tier chain read that tree through one storage interface.
rules:
  - id: immutable-tree-is-not-projection-manifest
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["TreeNode", "TreeEntry", "ProjectionManifest", "ProjectionEntry", "TreeWalk", "LevelWalk", "TierChain", "fetch_blobs", "fetch_at", "AbsentReason", "ItemAbsence", "Membership", "AskPolicy"]
    dedupe-of: "elohim/eprfs/eprfs-core/src/projection.rs (existing mutable local view)"
    why: >
      Reuse tree.rs and address.rs in eprfs-core for immutable,
      versioned, artifact-neutral values: byte names, raw leaves, modes, symlink target
      bytes, explicit external boundaries and build closure. ProjectionManifest and
      ProjectionEntry in projection.rs describe a mutable local view with adapter
      metadata; ProjectionAwareness carries local status. Neither is the canonical
      source or a tree CID. Git submodule pins, OIDs,
      index and worktree interpretation belong in brit's Git bridge; build recipes and
      hApp/Cargo packaging belong in rakia adapters. Migrate brit's BritCid
      through eprfs-core's explicit tag-42 BlobLink only with byte/CID conformance;
      a direct BlobCid alias can change serialized bytes. Preserve EprMeta v1 bytes.
      The view-walk seam reads the same tree: walk.rs (depth first) and level_walk.rs
      (one fetch_blobs per level, and `resolve_links` for many paths) share walk.rs's
      `expand`, its single-path steps `descend` and `link_of`, and tree.rs's `fetch_checked`,
      `accept_node` (verify, size, charge, decode) and `Budget`, so a new traversal adds no
      second verifier, matcher, step or budget. Every node and leaf fetch carries its root and
      path (`fetch_at`, `fetch_blobs`), so a forwarding tier can judge reach and membership. A
      tier's answer is `ItemAbsence` with ruling 7's three parts: `NotHeld` only when
      observed, `Withheld` for every other absence, and integrity is never degraded to
      absence: bytes this side verified and found bad, and a store's typed
      `EprfsError::Corrupt`, are errors in every walk and outrank a later refusal, while a
      remote tier's mere statement of integrity is an absence not established. Reach stays
      injected (`ReachGate`, `AskPolicy`; tier.rs and reach.rs): eprfs-core names no reach
      value, only the `ReachLevel` ordinal, and the tree and reach decisions a serving tier
      would otherwise make itself live here once (`Membership::of` and its exhaustive
      `absence` and `presence` answers, `check_membership`, `ReachLevel::narrower`, the parameterized `OpennessAsk` that every host and the
      TypeScript `standingPolicy` share through the vectors' `ask` section). A chain asks an
      outer tier in rounds of `ChainLimits::max_request_items`, the per-request bound a serving
      tier enforces (one default), and holds verified bytes for write-back only up to
      `ChainLimits::max_write_back_bytes`; it orders its tiers per request, for the root asked.
      Each kind of `impl EprfsStorage` runs its own
      eprfs-serve harness: a requester's composition (`TierChain`, a host's own) runs
      `assert_tier_contract`; an outer tier (an HTTP, blob-store or peer-transport tier, a
      loopback over a `TierEndpoint`) runs `assert_outer_tier_contract`; a leaf store runs
      `assert_leaf_contract`. A new tier class is a spec change. No I/O,
      runtime or clock in this crate (lib.rs `boundary`, rails R-E1 and R-E2).
---

# eprfs-core — portable tree contract

`address.rs` provides codec-aware `BlobCid` addressing and the explicit `BlobLink` wire
wrapper (tag-42 in DAG-CBOR, strings in JSON); existing `BlobCid` serialization is unchanged.
The wrapper is additive; existing serialized fields do not silently migrate.
`projection.rs` describes local projection entries. `tree.rs` owns the additive
version-1 shared tree and bounded verified closure loader; it does not reinterpret
`ProjectionManifest`. The tree is being qualified for publication as 0.1.2; source
presence is not proof that a standalone consumer can resolve it. Its bytes must
be stable across artifact adapters. A CID proves bytes, not publication standing or
which release head was elected; those decisions remain with content governance.

The view-walk seam (interfaces spec `placement-contract-interfaces-design` §A–§B):
`walk.rs` holds `View`, `TreePath`, `Coverage` and the depth-first walk with its three
queries; `level_walk.rs` the sans-I/O level-batched walk; `tier.rs` the tier identity,
records, absences, forwarding gate and `TierChain`; `reach.rs` the injected reach
policies; `view_decl.rs` the serializable `TreeView`; `derive.rs` the slice-4 derivation
types and ports. The serving side (`TierEndpoint`) and the tier contract harness live in
`eprfs-serve`, so a host can depend on them without depending on eprfs's adapters (R-E3).
Byte compatibility with `@elohim/eprfs` is pinned by `elohim/eprfs/fixtures/tree-vectors.json`,
which `tests/vectors.rs` recomputes.

## Rust style divergence: structured refusals by value

RS-GOV-04 records the existing size-lint decisions in `src/lib.rs` and `src/derive.rs`.
`TreeError` retains its inline CID and path; the derivation interfaces return the complete
`Unanswered` refusal and `DeriveOutcome` by value, once per task. Boxing these solely to satisfy
`result_large_err` or `large_enum_variant` would change the interfaces contract and add allocations
to refusals. The tradeoff is larger stack values. Scope: those attributes only; this does not
authorize unrelated large types. Evidence: the signatures and rationale immediately above the
attributes, and the interfaces contract's R7. Regression command: `just gate eprfs` checks the
consuming types and tests; the Rust style gate checks that these reasons stay attached. Responsible:
eprfs-core maintainers. Revisit when the interfaces change or measurements justify indirection.
